Now I understand the vulnerability. The crash is in `coap_split_uri_sub` called from `coap_get_uri_path`. The issue is in the IPv6 address reference parsing:

```c
if (len && *p == '[') {        /* IPv6 address reference */
    ++p;

    while (len && *q != ']') {
      ++q; --len;
    }
    ...
```

Wait, let me look more carefully. The bug is that `q` is used in the IPv6 parsing loop but `q` was set to `p` before. Let me trace through:

```c
q = p;  // q points to start of Uri-Host
if (len && *p == '[') {        /* IPv6 address reference */
    ++p;   // p now points past '['

    while (len && *q != ']') {   // BUG: uses *q but should use *p!
      ++q; --len;
    }
```

Wait, `q` was set to `p` before the `if`. So `q` points to `[`. Then `++p` moves p past `[`. But the while loop checks `*q != ']'` - q still points to `[`, so `*q` is `[` which is not `]`, so it increments `q` and decrements `len`. This continues until `len` becomes 0 or `*q == ']'`.

Actually wait, let me re-read. The bug is that the loop uses `*q` instead of `*p`. So `q` starts at `[` and moves forward. But `p` also moved forward by 1. So the loop reads `q` from position 1 (after `[`), while `p` is at position 1 too. Actually they're both at the same position initially after `++p`.

Hmm, let me think about this differently. The key issue is the read overflow. Let me look at the actual bug. The `while (len && *q != ']')` loop reads `*q` while `len > 0`. But `len` was decremented when we did `++p; --len;`? No wait, `++p` doesn't decrement len. Let me re-read:

```c
q = p;
if (len && *p == '[') {        /* IPv6 address reference */
    ++p;   // p moves past '[', len NOT decremented

    while (len && *q != ']') {
      ++q; --len;
    }
```

So `len` is not decremented when `++p` happens. The loop decrements `len` each iteration. So if the string is `[` followed by `len-1` more bytes, the loop will read `len` bytes total starting from `q` (which points at `[`). That's `len` bytes read, but the string only has `len` bytes. So it reads exactly `len` bytes - that's fine.

Wait, but the issue is that `q` starts at `[` and `p` starts at position after `[`. The loop reads `*q` and increments `q`. So it reads from position 0 (`[`), then position 1, etc. If the string is `[` + N bytes, and len = N+1, the loop reads N+1 bytes (positions 0 through N). Position N is the last byte. So no overflow there.

Hmm, let me think about the actual overflow. The crash is a READ 1 heap-buffer-overflow. Let me look at the git history to understand what changed.

